# encoder_hotspot_reprofile_2026-08-02 — provenance
# Companion to encoder_hotspot_reprofile_2026-08-02.md and its six TSVs.
#
# This RE-RUNS the methodology of encoder_hotspot_profile_2026-08-02.{md,meta}
# at a NEW baseline. That profile's ranking was taken against a 520.93 ms encode
# in which cnn_predict was 74.7 % self; KB-PERF-1 (6432e06) cached the CNN and
# the encode is now ~160 ms, so every SHARE in that ranking was stale and its
# levers 2-5 were explicitly arithmetic on the pre-fix self-costs. That file
# says "re-profile before ranking them again". This is that re-profile.
#
# NOTHING WAS OPTIMIZED IN THIS SESSION. This is a measurement task; every
# ceiling in the ranked table is arithmetic on a measured self-cost.

date                 2026-08-02
box                  Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GiB, macOS 26.5.2 (build 25F84, Darwin arm64)
repo commit          ab1748927cdeef0c40574401729942d404294b87   (worktree zenav1-aom--eprof2, detached at main)
  first profile was  0953fa758cf2612d962fb0c8429c01b9abb4251b
  changes between    6432e06 (KB-PERF-1, the CNN cache — the reason for this re-profile)
                     bc545df (test/bench re-pins)
                     ab17489 (KB-34, the nonrd non-square leaf walk — ALSO unblocked
                              cpu-used 9, which the first profile recorded as a refusal)
libaom oracle        upstream/ submodule 03087864cf4bea6abb0d28f95cf7843511413d8f  (v3.14.1)
                     — the SAME build as the first profile, deliberately.
rustc                1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)
C compiler           Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.1.1.101)
python               3.14.6
build flags          --release, NO -C target-cpu=native (runtime SIMD dispatch, what users get)
threading            single-threaded on both arms (the port has no threading; the oracle
                     libaom is built CONFIG_MULTITHREAD=0)
nice                 every measured run under `nice -n 19`

## BOX WAS NOT IDLE — read this before reading any absolute millisecond

A concurrent `zenmetrics` mac-worker fleet held the whole-box load average at
28-34 (of 12 cores) throughout, as it did during the first profile and the
CNN-cache study. It was another agent's live work and was NOT interfered with.
Handled, not hoped about:

* The control band is INTERLEAVED (port, C, port, C, …) over 9 independent
  invocations per arm and is reported FIRST, per docs/DIFFERENTIAL_PLAYBOOK.md
  §6. Spread: port 2.98 %, libaom-c 3.69 %, ratio 3.9 %.
* The PORT ARM WAS SAMPLED TWICE, independently, as a stability control
  (.stability.tsv). Every stage above 2 % of the window agrees within 5 %
  relative between the two runs; the worst is alloc/libc at −9.9 % (1.57 pp).
* libaom's own stage table is unchanged from the first profile to a tenth of a
  point (hog 24.92 vs 24.98, dsp:transform 9.62 vs 9.67, cnn 9.36 vs 9.33,
  trellis 8.30 vs 8.25) — same binary, same cell, two sessions. That is the
  method reproducing, not an assumption.

## Encoder arms (identical to benchmarks/xbench and to the first profile)

port        benchmarks/xbench/drv-aom          `EncodeCell::port_encode_with(&bootstrap, default knobs)`
                                                timed region = the port's whole frame encode; the
                                                UNTIMED C sequence-header bootstrap is structural
                                                (the port never authors a sequence header).
libaom-c    benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c timed region = aom_codec_encode + drain + flush + drain;
                                                enc_init/destroy/img_alloc excluded. The first profile
                                                MEASURED the untimed fraction at 0.15 % (200 reps),
                                                so no correction is applied.

## Profile cell — unchanged from the first profile, for continuity

1024x1024 photo, cq-level 44, cpu-used 6, ALLINTRA (usage 2), 8-bit 4:2:0,
single tile, single thread. Source: $HOME/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv, produced by
`python3 scripts/xbench.py prep` (Lanczos3 square:1024 downscale of
codec-corpus clic2025/final-test/8426ed22...png), byte-identical to the file the
2026-08-01 cross-encoder study and the first profile used.
Both arms emit the SAME 4472-byte stream (4458-byte coded frame) at this cell,
and at all 13 breadth cells `port_bytes == c_bytes`.

## Tooling — reused unchanged from the first profile

profiler             /usr/bin/sample, 1 ms interval, 60 s window, started 5 s after launch
                     so the .yuv read, the C bootstrap and the warm-up encode are outside it.
                     45 541 samples (port run 2), 45 846 (port run 1), 46 219 (libaom-c).
  * WALL-CLOCK SAMPLER, not an instruction counter. No valgrind on Apple Silicon,
    so the deterministic-Ir method the decode-side gate3 profiles used is not on
    the table. IPC and cache behaviour are folded into every number here.
  * Does NOT expand inlined frames — self cost is charged to the outermost
    NON-inlined function. See the .md's "Attribution limits" section for the six
    specific rows this damages and for which of them are cross-checked by a
    measurement that does not use the profiler at all.
  * libaom.a carries no -g, so its `static` functions symbolicate to the nearest
    preceding exported symbol. Globals (nearly all the top of its profile) are exact.
  * The port arm is a CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=2 build (+ dsymutil). Debug info
    does not change codegen: BYTE-IDENTICAL .obu, 158.3-158.8 ms vs the plain
    release build's 159.78 ms — inside the 2.98 % control spread.

rollup               scripts/eprof_rollup.py    (unchanged)
alignment            scripts/eprof_align.py     (unchanged)
caller attribution   scripts/eprof_callers.py   (unchanged)
alloc census         crates/aom-bench/examples/eprof_alloc.rs      (unchanged)
CNN per-call bench   crates/aom-bench/examples/eprof_cnn_bench.rs  (unchanged)
control band         scripts/eprof_control.sh   (unchanged)
breadth              scripts/eprof_breadth.sh   (unchanged)
sampler wrapper      scripts/eprof_sample.sh    — ONE change, made here: the `port-dbg`
                     arm's binary path is now overridable via $PORT_DBG. A STALE
                     debug build from the first profile's session was sitting at the
                     hardcoded default path ($HOME/tmp/eprof-dbg-target/release/drv-aom,
                     built 03:37 at the PRE-CACHE commit); sampling it would have
                     silently re-measured the old encoder. Not hypothetical — it was
                     found there.

## Exact commands

  git worktree add --detach ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--eprof2 main
  git -C ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--eprof2 submodule update --init

  nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p zenav1-aom-sys-ref          # the C oracle
  nice -n 19 cc -O2 -o benchmarks/xbench/target/drv_libaom \
      benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c -Iupstream -Iupstream/build \
      -Lupstream/build -laom -lm -lc++
  (cd benchmarks/xbench && nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  (cd benchmarks/xbench && CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$HOME/tmp/eprof2-dbg-target \
      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=2 nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  dsymutil ~/tmp/eprof2-dbg-target/release/drv-aom
  nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p zenav1-aom-bench \
      --example eprof_alloc --example eprof_cnn_bench --example eprof_cnn_verify

  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_control.sh 9 ~/tmp/eprof2_control.tsv
  PORT_DBG=$HOME/tmp/eprof2-dbg-target/release/drv-aom \
    nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh port-dbg 500 60 ~/tmp/eprof2_port.sample.txt 5
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh libaom-c 1500 60 ~/tmp/eprof2_libaomc.sample.txt 5
  PORT_DBG=$HOME/tmp/eprof2-dbg-target/release/drv-aom \
    nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh port-dbg 500 60 ~/tmp/eprof2_port2.sample.txt 5
  python3 scripts/eprof_rollup.py ~/tmp/eprof2_port2.sample.txt   --arm port     --rules rust --tsv ~/tmp/eprof2_port2.tsv
  python3 scripts/eprof_rollup.py ~/tmp/eprof2_libaomc.sample.txt --arm libaom-c --rules c    --tsv ~/tmp/eprof2_c.tsv
  python3 scripts/eprof_align.py --port ~/tmp/eprof2_port2.tsv --port-ms 166.017 \
                                 --c    ~/tmp/eprof2_c.tsv     --c-ms     47.647
  python3 scripts/eprof_callers.py ~/tmp/eprof2_port2.sample.txt --leaf '<allocator class>'
  python3 scripts/eprof_callers.py ~/tmp/eprof2_port2.sample.txt --leaf 'mach_absolute_time|clock_gettime|gettimeofday'
  python3 scripts/eprof_callers.py ~/tmp/eprof2_libaomc.sample.txt --leaf 'sigprocmask|sigaltstack|mach_absolute_time'
  ./target/release/examples/eprof_alloc     1024 1024 44 6 ~/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv
  ./target/release/examples/eprof_cnn_bench 1024 1024        ~/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv 2000
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_breadth.sh ~/tmp/eprof2_breadth.tsv

## Denominators used in the aligned table

port       166.017 ms/encode  (median of the 500 timed reps taken DURING port sample run 2)
libaom-c    47.647 ms/encode  (median of the 1500 timed reps taken DURING the libaom-c run)
ratio during profiling 3.484x; ratio in the clean interleaved control 3.343x.
`sample` suspends the target to walk stacks, which inflates the port arm's
during-profile median by ~4 % (port run 1 during-profile median was 163.632 ms).
The .md quotes the CONTROL-BAND ratio for the headline and the during-profile
medians for the aligned table, so each number is paired with the run it came from.

## Cross-checks that do NOT use the profiler

* CNN call count: 256 per frame, 1.00 per 64x64 SB, on all five load-bearing
  exact-size counters (eprof_alloc). libaom's is 256. (Two of the seven watch
  sizes, 64 B and 80 B, are shared with unrelated call sites and are NOT usable
  as CNN counters — they report 71 908 and 1 852. The five that ARE single-site
  move in lockstep.)
* CNN per-call cost: 146 171 ns port / 128 692 ns libaom `_c` / 17 059 ns libaom
  NEON (eprof_cnn_bench, 7 rounds x 2000 iters, outputs compared first).
  256 x 146.17 µs = 37.42 ms vs the profile's 40.02 ms (6.9 % apart);
  256 x 17.06 µs = 4.37 ms vs the profile's 4.46 ms (2.1 % apart).
* Forward-transform lane width: read from BOTH sources, not inferred.
  upstream/av1/encoder/arm/av1_fwd_txfm2d_neon.c:401-1476 — every fdct/fadst/
  fidentity kernel takes `const int16x8_t *`. The port's
  crates/aom-dsp/src/transform/simd/mod.rs:574,:685 are
  `#[magetypes(define(i32x8), ...)]` and widen16() on load. lowbd16.rs contains
  INVERSE kernels only.
* Forward-pass scratch memset: profiler attribution (try_fwd_col_pass +124 ->
  _platform_memset at mod.rs:0) AND source (mod.rs:592,:703 flat
  `[i32x8::zero(t); 64]` x2 = 4 KiB/call, vs the tiered {8,16,64} at :430-443,
  :784-797, :994-1000) AND the in-repo comment at lowbd16.rs:132 that states the
  tiering exists because "a flat 64-entry array zero-init is a memset that
  dominates the small transforms".
* Stage-table internal consistency: the sum of the two rows that moved since the
  first profile (cnn −342.33, partition-search −3.17) is −345.50 against a
  measured total gap move of −345.15 ms, across sixteen independently-sampled
  stages.

## Committed data files

  .control.tsv         9 invocations x 2 arms, per-invocation median + all 7 sample NS + bytes
  .stages.tsv          the aligned stage table (eprof_align.py --out)
  .symbols.tsv         BOTH arms' rollups concatenated. TRUNCATED to symbols with
                       self_samples >= 10 to stay under the repo's 30 KB file rule;
                       that keeps 99.07 % of the port's and 98.97 % of libaom's self
                       samples. Every stage row is present untruncated, so the stage
                       totals in the .md are reproducible from this file; the dropped
                       rows are individually <= 0.022 % of a window and are below the
                       reproducibility floor measured in .stability.tsv. Regenerate
                       the full lists with the eprof_rollup.py commands above.
  .breadth.tsv         13 cells, both arms, with the exact CNN call count per cell
  .alloc_callers.tsv   full caller attribution of the allocator/memset/memcpy leaf class
  .stability.tsv       port sample run 1 vs run 2, per stage — the sampling control

